#1 Inline syntax

Inline constructs describe meaning within a paragraph without introducing new block structure. StructMark uses `[content]` to make formatting boundaries explicit. The examples below use StructMark syntax, not another language's `@command` syntax.

#2 Emphasis

`*[content]` is strong emphasis, `/[content]` is italic emphasis and `/*[content]` combines both. The syntax supports nesting.

```sm
*[Important text]
/[Secondary emphasis]
/*[Bold and italic]
*[Outer /[inner]]
```

#2 Text decoration

Underline, deletion, highlight, superscript and subscript are separate constructs with different meanings.

```sm
-[Underline]
~[Deleted text]
=[Highlighted text]
x^[2] + y^[2]
H_[2]O
```

#2 Inline code and math

Backticks preserve literal source. Math uses `$TeX$`; its typesetting depends on the renderer.

```sm
This is `*[not strong]` source code.
Formula: $E=mc^2$
```

#2 Links, footnotes and references

A link uses `[label](target)`. A semantic `&ref[id]` refers to an identified node and may require workspace validation.

```sm
[Home](https://example.com)
Read &ref[intro] for details.
Footnote[^note]
```

#2 Escaping and boundaries

Pay attention to the context around reserved punctuation. Unclosed constructs should produce diagnostics rather than consume unrelated document content.
